---
/**
 * hospitality / AreaBK — "Villa Marindu" WHAT IS NEARBY (design element
 * data-component="SERVICE-AREA").
 *
 * ROLE: the page's one orientation panel. A whole-villa let is sold partly on
 * what is reachable from the gate, so this section names the places the owner
 * listed and — where the owner supplied one — how long each takes. It is the
 * only section in the template drawn as a CONTAINED CARD rather than a band.
 *
 * DESIGN VALUES, lifted verbatim from "Villa Marindu Landing.dc.html":
 *   section   padding 80px 0 (the design writes 80px 34px 0; the 34px is its
 *             own page gutter, which is .wrap here)
 *   panel     background #141A18 · 1px #262E2B ALL ROUND · radius 3px ·
 *             padding 44px 40px            → --night-2 / --rule / --r-lg
 *   head      flex · align-items flex-end · space-between · gap 26px ·
 *             flex-wrap wrap · margin-bottom 26px
 *   eyebrow   10px / .44em caps / #B99552 / 600            → .kick
 *   h2        Prata 400 · clamp(24px,2.9vw,36px) · 1.16 · margin .26em 0 0 ·
 *             #F4F1EA                                      → .h2 + .na-h2
 *   note      14.5px / 1.7 / #767C76 / max-width 34ch / margin 0 → --muted
 *   grid      repeat(3, minmax(0,1fr)) · gap 0 34px (no ROW gap — the hairline
 *             under each row is the separation)
 *   row       flex · baseline · gap 12px · padding 12px 0 ·
 *             border-bottom 1px #212926  → the wrapper's .rowrule, exactly
 *   place     14.5px / #EFEAE0                             → --bone
 *   time      margin-left auto · 13px / #B99552 / nowrap    → --accent
 * Every colour above is a wrapper token (R1); not one hex is written in this
 * file.
 *
 * NOT .band-panel, deliberately. The wrapper's band lifts a FULL-BLEED section
 * onto --night-2 with top and bottom hairlines only. The design draws a boxed
 * card inside the page gutter with a border on all four sides and a 3px radius,
 * so this file builds .na-panel instead. Using the band would have been the
 * cheap read of "background #141A18" and it is a different shape.
 *
 * NOT .fig for the travel time, deliberately. .fig is the serif brass figure
 * used for rates and numerals elsewhere in this template; the design sets NO
 * font-family on the time, so it inherits Figtree. It is brass sans 13px, not
 * Prata, and that is the design.
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS (all declared in components/editor/genericContentSchema.ts,
 * group "area" — a data-field on any other path is a dead hook no sidebar
 * input owns)
 *   area.tag                  — the eyebrow
 *   area.headline             — set:html, so an owner can place the template's
 *                               signature italic-brass <em> anywhere inside
 *                               their own sentence
 *   area.body                 — the note beside the headline
 *   area.rows.${i}.place      — the primary list: place + travel time
 *   area.rows.${i}.time
 *   area.places.${i}          — the FALLBACK list, a list of plain STRINGS.
 *                               Read only when area.rows has no usable row;
 *                               rendered place-only, with no time element at
 *                               all rather than an empty brass slot
 *
 * area.body vs area.sub. area.body is the canonical path the schema owns and
 * the only one hooked. area.sub is READ (never hooked) so a site that stored a
 * paragraph there before the schema settled still shows it; the first edit
 * writes area.body, which then wins, so the two can never disagree on screen.
 * Hooking area.sub instead would emit a dead hook — v3 gates inline editing on
 * isSchemaEditablePath and the schema does not declare it.
 *
 * ANTI-FABRICATION (R2). Not one word here is written by this file. The
 * design's own list ("Private cove · 4 min walk", "Lio Airport · 25 min") is
 * the DESIGNER filling in their own template — those are distance claims about
 * a property nobody has visited, so none of them ships. A row with a time but
 * no place is DROPPED: a lone brass "25 min" asserts a journey to nowhere. A
 * row with a place and no time renders the place alone, and the time element is
 * not emitted at all — every guard sits outside its tag, so an empty value
 * removes the ELEMENT, never leaving a bare hairline or an empty brass figure.
 * The head is guarded the same way at BOTH levels: the whole .na-head is only
 * emitted when the owner left a tag, a headline or a note (otherwise its 26px
 * bottom margin would open a hole above the list), and its left half is only
 * emitted when there is a tag or a headline (an empty flex item under
 * space-between would push the note to the right edge of the panel).
 *
 * SECTION GATE (R5). lib/derive-content-defaults.ts hard-codes area.tag to
 * 'Service area' and, for any site with a city, area.headline to `Serving
 * ${city}` AND area.places to `[city]` — on essentially every site we ship. So
 * the gate counts BODY content only, and discounts that single derived entry:
 *   · any usable area.rows row (the schema ships area.rows EMPTY, so a filled
 *     one can only have come from a person), OR
 *   · TWO OR MORE area.places entries (the owner named a second place), OR
 *   · an owner-written area.body.
 * A `places.length > 0` test would have shipped this panel on nearly every
 * build with one row echoing the property's own city — the address again,
 * dressed as a neighbourhood guide.
 *
 * KNOWN EDGE, flagged rather than hidden: when the gate opens on area.body
 * alone and area.places is still the derived `[city]`, that one city row is
 * printed. It is true (it is where the villa is) and it matches the shipped
 * behaviour of the sibling AreaBJ, so the family stays consistent — but it is
 * the one place where a derived value reaches the page, and it is a one-line
 * change to require two places before the list draws at all.
 *
 * LIST INDEXES (R6). data-field always carries the ORIGINAL array index, so
 * dropping a blank row 2 cannot misdirect an edit of row 3 into row 2. There is
 * no decorative numeral in this section, so no display index is needed.
 *
 * SHAPE TOLERANCE. area.rows is a list of objects; a row that arrived as a
 * plain string is read as its place and still hooked at area.rows.${i}.place,
 * so the first edit heals the row onto the schema key instead of writing a
 * second shape.
 *
 * NOT RENDERED, deliberately:
 *   - No id on the section, and no links of any kind. The design gives
 *     SERVICE-AREA no id and no anchor, and this page's ids are about /
 *     services / gallery / location / faq / main. Because nothing here emits an
 *     href, this file carries NO onPage() anchor-remap helper — the copy that
 *     HeaderBK / HeroBK / HowBK / CtaBandBK / FooterBK share would be dead code
 *     here. That is an omission on purpose, not an oversight.
 *   - No row count is asserted in markup or copy. Three tracks because the
 *     design is three tracks; four or eleven owner-typed places lay out on the
 *     same tracks without a hole.
 *   - No map, no distance unit, no "within X km" line. We have no such data.
 *
 * ACCESSIBILITY (R9). No image and no plate here, so no role="img". Nothing in
 * this section is decorative, so nothing is aria-hidden — the place and the
 * time are both content and both announced. The list is a real <ul> AND carries
 * role="list": the design's rows have no markers, and `list-style: none` is
 * exactly what makes Safari/VoiceOver drop list semantics, so without the role
 * the count would not reach assistive tech at all. The hairline is the only
 * visual separator and it carries no meaning of its own.
 *
 * CONTRAST (sRGB, the design's palette on the --night-2 panel):
 *   --bone on --night-2 14.6 (the place) · --accent on --night-2 6.4 (the time)
 *   --muted on --night-2 4.13 — BELOW AA, and the design's own value. The
 *   wrapper's frontmatter documents the same trade for every --muted run in
 *   this template: it carries only the note beside a headline that already says
 *   the same thing. Lifting --muted is one line in PageBK, it is the owner's
 *   palette, and this file ships the design rather than silently re-deriving it.
 *
 * SCOPING (R3). One scoped <style>, no is:global. The headline is fed with
 * set:html and scoped rules cannot reach elements a set:html creates — which is
 * why there is no italic-brass rule here: PageBK styles `:where(.h2 em)` from
 * its global block, so an owner's <em> is emphasised there and a scoped
 * duplicate would be dead CSS.
 */
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content } = Astro.props;

const area = content.area || {};
const tag = area.tag || '';
const headline = area.headline || '';
const body = area.body || area.sub || '';

type Row = { place: string; time: string; placeField: string; timeField: string };

// PRIMARY: area.rows — place + travel time. An object row is canonical; a
// string row is tolerated and still hooked at .place so the first edit heals it.
const rawRows: any[] = Array.isArray(area.rows) ? area.rows : [];
const timedRows: Row[] = rawRows
  .map((r: any, i: number): Row => {
    const o = r && typeof r === 'object' ? r : { place: r };
    return {
      place: String(o.place ?? '').trim(),
      time: String(o.time ?? '').trim(),
      placeField: `area.rows.${i}.place`,
      timeField: `area.rows.${i}.time`,
    };
  })
  // A time with no place is a journey to nowhere — drop the row entirely.
  .filter((r) => r.place.length > 0);

// FALLBACK: area.places — plain STRINGS, seven templates already read them as
// strings, so nothing is reshaped here. Place-only rows; no time element.
const rawPlaces: any[] = Array.isArray(area.places) ? area.places : [];
const placeRows: Row[] = rawPlaces
  .map((p: any, i: number): Row => ({
    place: String(p ?? '').trim(),
    time: '',
    placeField: `area.places.${i}`,
    timeField: '',
  }))
  .filter((r) => r.place.length > 0);

const rows: Row[] = timedRows.length > 0 ? timedRows : placeRows;
// See SECTION GATE in the block above. Never `rows.length > 0`.
const show = timedRows.length > 0 || placeRows.length >= 2 || Boolean(body);
---
{show && (
  <section class="na">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="na-panel">
        {(tag || headline || body) && (
          <div class="na-head reveal">
            {(tag || headline) && (
              <div class="na-title">
                {tag && <span class="kick" data-field="area.tag">{tag}</span>}
                {headline && <h2 class="h2 na-h2" data-field="area.headline" set:html={headline}></h2>}
              </div>
            )}
            {body && <p class="na-note" data-field="area.body">{body}</p>}
          </div>
        )}

        {rows.length > 0 && (
          <ul class="na-list reveal" role="list" data-delay="1">
            {rows.map((r) => (
              <li class="rowrule na-row">
                <span class="na-place" data-field={r.placeField}>{r.place}</span>
                {r.time && <span class="na-time" data-field={r.timeField}>{r.time}</span>}
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        )}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
)}
<style>
  /* Design: padding 80px 0. Its siblings are padding-top-only and PageBK gives
     every section symmetric padding, so the shared rhythm already supplies the
     trailing gap; only the 80px ceiling is taken here, on the shared curve so it
     never crushes on a phone. */
  .na { padding: clamp(60px, 8vw, 80px) 0; }

  /* Design: #141A18 · 1px #262E2B all round · 3px · 44px 40px. NOT .band-panel
     — see the note in the frontmatter. The padding clamps resolve to exactly
     44px / 40px at every width the design was drawn at, and only give way below
     ~730px where 40px of gutter inside a 92vw wrap starts eating the rows. */
  .na-panel {
    background-color: var(--night-2);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: var(--r-lg);
    padding: clamp(26px, 6vw, 44px) clamp(20px, 5vw, 40px);
  }

  /* Design: flex · flex-end · space-between · gap 26px · wrap · mb 26px. */
  .na-head {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-end;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 26px;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    margin-bottom: 26px;
  }
  /* min-width:0 on both halves so a long unbroken owner string shrinks its half
     instead of widening the panel (R8). */
  .na-title { min-width: 0; }

  /* The eyebrow is owner-typed and set in 10px caps at .44em tracking, so one
     unbroken word is unusually wide for its character count (R8). Same guard
     HowBK puts on its own .kick. */
  .kick { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }

  /* Design: Prata 400 · clamp(24px,2.9vw,36px) · 1.16 · margin .26em 0 0. This
     headline is DELIBERATELY smaller than the wrapper's .h2 (clamp(31px,4vw,
     54px)) — the design sizes the panel head below a full section head. The
     wrapper's rule is a :where(), so this class wins with no !important.
     max-width is left INHERITED at the primitive's 24ch: the design sets none,
     and 24ch is what keeps the head a two-part row instead of pushing the note
     onto a line of its own. */
  .na-h2 {
    font-size: clamp(24px, 2.9vw, 36px);
    line-height: 1.16;
    margin: 0.26em 0 0;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* Design: 14.5px / 1.7 / #767C76 / 34ch / margin 0. Not the wrapper's .body
     primitive, which is --grey at clamp(14.5px,1.3vw,15px) — a different colour
     and a different scale. Specificity (0,2,0) with the scope class, so it beats
     the wrapper's (0,1,1) bare-paragraph reset. */
  .na-note {
    margin: 0;
    min-width: 0;
    max-width: 34ch;
    font-size: 14.5px;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--muted);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* Design: repeat(3, minmax(0,1fr)) · gap 0 34px. The ZERO row gap is the
     design: the hairline under each row is the separation, and a row gap would
     open a space between a rule and the row under it. minmax(0,1fr) rather than
     1fr so a long place name cannot widen its track and push the page sideways. */
  .na-list {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 0 34px;
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
  }
  /* Flex, baseline, 12px gap, 12px 0 padding and the --rule-soft hairline all
     come from the wrapper's .rowrule, which is character-for-character this
     design's row. Only list-item chrome is reset here. */
  .na-row { list-style: none; }

  .na-place {
    font-size: 14.5px;
    color: var(--bone);
    min-width: 0;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
  /* Design: margin-left auto · 13px · #B99552 · nowrap. No font-family: the
     design leaves it on Figtree, so this is NOT .fig. */
  .na-time {
    margin-left: auto;
    font-size: 13px;
    color: var(--accent);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  /* R8 · this section owns its own grid. Three tracks are the design and they
     must not survive past 900px, where a 34px gutter leaves a place name and its
     time fighting for the same line. */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .na-list { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 0 28px; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 620px) {
    .na-list { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
    .na-head { gap: 18px; margin-bottom: 20px; }
    .na-note { max-width: none; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    .na-place { font-size: 14px; }
    /* Release the design's nowrap at the one width where a long owner-typed
       time ("about 35 minutes by tricycle") would otherwise push the viewport
       sideways. Above 520px the tracks are wide enough for it to hold. */
    .na-time { white-space: normal; overflow-wrap: anywhere; text-align: right; }
  }
</style>
